  "configuration_id": "config_006",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_005 (RNA-only, multi-profile, scVI n200, avgpool) achieved the best performance so far (Acc 0.650, F1 0.628), a large jump over its single-profile counterpart config_004 (Acc 0.520). This confirms multi-profile is the dominant design lever. However, we have only tested one aggregation method (avgpool) in the multi-profile setting. Question: Does transformer-based aggregation capture inter-profile interactions that simple averaging misses? config_006 changes only the aggregation from avgpool to transformer while holding encoder (scVI n200) and profile setting (multi) constant, giving a clean A/B comparison against config_005. Why this is the best next experiment: Aggregation is a fundamental architectural choice that affects every multi-profile configuration downstream. If transformer aggregation provides a meaningful gain, it signals that profile-order/interaction information is exploitable and should be carried into all subsequent multi-profile and multimodal experiments. If it does not, we confirm avgpool is sufficient and can redirect budget toward encoder comparison (e.g., scGPT) or multimodal fusion. This single experiment reduces the most impactful uncertainty with minimal confounding."
}

